<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("radius") %>
<% radii = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("radius") %>
<% local_radii = radii.reject(&:zero?) %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 3 asks what spatial locality buys. A neighbourhood radius decides who a
  cell can interact with: at
  <span class="mono"><%= local_radii.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  a tape only ever meets its neighbours, while
  <span class="mono">radius = 0</span> is the well-mixed world — a partner drawn uniformly
  from the whole torus, the infinity arm of the design record. Locality is supposed to cut
  both ways: a tight neighbourhood shelters a new lineage from the soup long enough to take
  hold, and it also starves it of the variety a copy needs, so a tight world should be
  slower to produce anything at all.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  A single world shape, <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> seeds per arm and <%= epochs %>
  epochs per run, with the radius as the only parameter that moves:
  <span class="mono"><%= radii.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>. The
  dependent variables are both observables of DESIGN §1.2 — the transition epoch and the
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> census — read from the samples themselves in
  the tables below.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  Open: most of the grid is terminal but no claim is made here yet. The shape so far is that
  connectivity buys emergence. The widest local neighbourhood and the well-mixed arm both
  produce transitions that the detector and the census agree on; the two tightest arms
  produce none, and each of them has instead a single seed that collapses
  <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> with a census of zero — the same disagreement class
  the <%= link_to "mutation-rate", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> and
  <%= link_to "world-size", finding_path("world-size-scaling") %> sweeps report, a
  low-entropy attractor that is not a replicator by the locked test.
</p>
<p>
  Two readings of that are still open, and this sweep alone cannot separate them: either a
  tight neighbourhood is genuinely hostile to a replicator, or it is merely slower than the
  budget, in which case the arms that show nothing are censored rather than negative. The
  arms and the per-run numbers are in the phase diagram and the evidence tables below, which
  read the runs live.
</p>
